Wait for Keystone and Placement while nova-compute starts

`ComputeManager.__init__` (nova/compute/manager.py) creates its
`SchedulerReportClient` while nova-compute starts. The client's constructor
builds the openstacksdk Placement proxy through
`nova.utils.get_sdk_adapter`, and its version discovery takes a token from
Keystone and asks Placement for its versions. Any error there reaches
`nova.cmd.compute.main` unhandled and ends the process. A refused or dropped
connection to Keystone raises keystoneauth's ConnectFailure, a 5xx from
Keystone raises its HttpServerError, and a Placement that does not answer
raises openstacksdk's NotSupported ("exists but does not have any supported
versions"). Upstream master (b82fedba6b, 2026-11-03) still creates the
client this way.

The e2e-chaos-random job of
https://github.com/C5C3/cobaltcore/actions/runs/41903435759 (seed
41903435759) hit this. Its step s04 killed the fake compute pod and the
MariaDB pod together at 21:12:53. The new nova-compute started at 21:12:59
and asked Keystone for a token at 21:13:00, while Keystone could not reach
its database. The connection closed without an answer at 21:14:00, and
nova-compute ended with "Unhandled error: ConnectFailure". The kubelet
restarted the container at 21:14:04, the second start succeeded, and the
no-restarts check counted the restart. A standalone run of the published
image's `SchedulerReportClient()` against a closed port, a Keystone that
answers 500, and a Placement that is down raises the three errors above
on the first attempt.

`Service.__init__` (nova/service.py) waits for nova-conductor just before it
creates the manager, as long as it takes, because "If we allow an exception
to be raised, the service will shut down." The Placement client now gets the
same wait. `compute_utils.call_until_placement_answers` retries its creation
on keystoneauth's RetriableConnectionFailure (ConnectFailure and
ConnectTimeout), on a 500, 502, 503 or 504, and on NotSupported. It logs a
warning for each failed attempt and waits 2, 4 and 8 seconds, then 16
seconds between attempts, with no attempt limit. Every other error is a
configuration nova-compute cannot wait out and still ends the process on
the first attempt: a 401, a missing endpoint or auth plugin, a TLS error, a
501.

The wait has no bound, unlike the 254 seconds of patches 0002 and 0003.
Those waits hold an instance lock and an RPC worker. This one holds
nothing, since nova-compute serves no RPC before its manager exists. A
bound would only trade the wait for a restart, and the kubelet's back-off,
which grows to five minutes, would then delay the start after Keystone is
back. A Placement that has no supported version at all keeps nova-compute
waiting with a warning every 16 seconds instead of restarting it.

Only nova-compute creates the client at start. The API, the conductor and
the scheduler take the shared client from `report_client_singleton()` on
first use.

Tests: CallUntilPlacementAnswersTestCase (nova/tests/unit/compute/
test_utils.py) covers the helper. Seven unreachable answers are retried with
the waits above, 40 failures in a row do not end the wait, and six
configuration errors reach the caller on the first attempt.
test_init_waits_for_keystone_and_placement (nova/tests/unit/compute/
test_compute_mgr.py) fails without the patch with ConnectFailure.
test_init_fails_on_rejected_placement_credentials pins that a 401 still
ends the start. No upstream test pins the construction of the client in
`ComputeManager.__init__`.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_nova.sh asserts in the built image that
`ComputeManager()` survives a refused Keystone and a Placement without
versions and keeps the client of the third attempt.

Applies to nova 34.0.0 (2026.2).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

